  "id": "landmine-press",
  "name": "Landmine Press",
  "definition": "A narrow unilateral standing split-stance press using an Olympic bar whose far end is fixed to a landmine pivot. The moving sleeve begins close to the working-side deltoid and follows the bar's constrained rotational arc while the shoulder flexes and the elbow extends. Zhao et al. directly establish the apparatus, right-arm left-foot-forward stance, total bar-plus-plate load accounting, twenty-to-thirty-five-kilogram test range, and three-repetition power-test topology. The everyday strength record preserves that strict geometry but uses controlled repetitions and an added-plates-only value that is comparable only on the same bar, pivot, and setup; Szelog supports landmine pressing for strength while the logging convention is an explicit product adaptation rather than a measure of total resistance. Zhao's system tracks bar endpoints rather than human segments, so the sagittal shoulder-flexion model, diagonal push classification, strict no-leg-drive execution, mirrored left-arm prescription, and muscle hierarchy remain disclosed catalog adaptations. No scapular prime action is inferred: serratus and the trapezius regions receive stability credit only. Half-kneeling, tall-kneeling, bilateral, two-hand, rotational, squat-to-press, explosive throw, Viking-press, free-bar, cable, machine, and torso-lean variants remain outside this contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "ackland-2008-shoulder-moment-arms",
    "holzbaur-2005-upper-extremity",
    "murray-1995-elbow-forearm-moment-arms",
    "zhao-2026-landmine-press-kinematics",
    "nsca-2016-landmine-press"
  ],
